      'Dolby\'s Atmos cinema specification allows up to 128 simultaneous audio elements delivered to a theater, combining traditional bed channels with dynamically positioned objects that the in-theater renderer maps onto whatever speaker configuration that auditorium has, from a modest multiplex to a flagship auditorium with dozens of speakers. This object-based approach lets the exact same Atmos master scale correctly across wildly different room sizes without a separate mix for each configuration, unlike fixed-channel-count formats like Dolby Digital 5.1 or DTS. Because the number is a hard specification limit, mixers must carefully manage element budgets on dense action or music sequences, sometimes collapsing less important sounds into bed channels.',
