      'The Bayer pattern, patented by Kodak\'s Bryce Bayer in 1976, uses a repeating 2x2 block containing two green-filtered photosites diagonally opposite each other, plus one red and one blue, because the human eye is most sensitive to green light. Doubling green sampling density improves perceived sharpness and reduces luminance noise relative to an equal RGB split. A camera\'s image processor then demosaics the missing color information at each photosite from its neighbors to reconstruct a full-color image, a step that can introduce characteristic artifacts like color moiré. Some cinema cameras use alternative sensor designs to avoid Bayer demosaicing tradeoffs, but the vast majority remain Bayer-based.',
