      'Gaussian splatting represents a scene as millions of 3D Gaussian primitives, soft ellipsoidal blobs with position, scale, orientation, opacity, and view-dependent color, rather than a neural network function as in NeRF. It renders novel viewpoints extremely fast by directly \'splatting\' these Gaussians onto the screen rather than requiring dense per-pixel neural network queries. This combination of high visual quality and real-time rendering speed made it attractive for virtual production, game engines, and VFX pipelines exploring photogrammetry-style capture. It can be edited and relit more tractably than a NeRF\'s opaque neural weights. Unlike a polygon mesh, a Gaussian splat scene has no explicit connected surface geometry.',
