      0,
      'NTSC color video\'s frame rate is not exactly 30fps but 29.97fps, a deliberate reduction introduced in 1953. A timecode counter incrementing in exact whole-number 30-frame-per-second steps would drift noticeably ahead of true wall-clock elapsed time over a long program. Drop-frame timecode compensates by skipping two frame numbers, not two actual frames, every minute except every tenth minute, keeping the timecode display closely synchronized with real elapsed time, which matters for broadcast programs that must hit exact clock times and ad-break durations. Non-drop-frame timecode drifts against real clock time by roughly 3.6 seconds over a full hour, an error broadcast engineering can\'t tolerate for scheduled live television.',
